Pointer Planetarium is MIT licensed (see LICENSE). Some of the data services it uses
at run time do not allow commercial use (adsb.fi), so Pointer Planetarium itself is
free, with no ads or purchases. It includes or derives data from:

- Yale Bright Star Catalogue, 5th Revised Edition (Hoffleit and Warren
  1991), via CDS VizieR catalog V/50. Positions and magnitudes, trimmed to
  magnitude 5.5 and repacked (src/pkjs/stars_data.js).
- IAU Catalog of Star Names, IAU Working Group on Star Names (WGSN),
  released by the IAU under Creative Commons Attribution, as mirrored by
  E. Mamajek (2022-04-04 edition).
- Constellation boundaries: N. G. Roman (1987), "Identification of a
  Constellation from a Position", PASP 99, 695; CDS VizieR catalog VI/42.
- Constellation stick figures: Marc van der Sluys, ConstellationLines
  (https://github.com/MarcvdSluys/ConstellationLines,
  DOI 10.5281/zenodo.10397192), Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
  International. Modified: matched to this app's star catalog, and lines
  broken where a star has no position.
- Star distances: Hipparcos parallaxes (ESA 1997, The Hipparcos and Tycho
  Catalogues, ESA SP-1200), via CDS VizieR catalog I/239, where good to
  20%.
- resources/data/sky.bin, the watch's copy of the stars, names and
  boundaries, is derived from the three above (tools/make_watch_sky.js).
- The fun facts (src/pkjs/facts.js) are written for this app; their
  numbers are from NASA fact sheets, the IAU and aircraft makers' figures.
- World Magnetic Model 2025, NOAA National Centers for Environmental
  Information and the British Geological Survey (public domain). The
  evaluator in src/pkjs/wmm.js is ported from magvar 2.2.0
  (https://github.com/dpyeates/magvar), MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025
  Darren Yeates.
- Planet and Moon positions follow Paul Schlyter, "How to compute planetary
  positions" (https://stjarnhimlen.se/comp/ppcomp.html), after van Flandern
  and Pulkkinen (1980). Mercury and Venus magnitudes follow Mallama and
  Hilton (2018).

- The planet and Moon pictures (resources/images/planet_*.png, moon.png,
  from the photos in art/src by tools/make_art.py; art/manifest.json links
  each source) are converted from public domain NASA images: Mercury,
  NASA/JHUAPL/Carnegie Institution of Washington (MESSENGER, PIA11364);
  Venus, NASA/JPL-Caltech (Mariner 10, PIA23791); Mars, NASA and the
  Hubble Heritage Team (STScI/AURA); Jupiter, NASA/JPL/University of
  Arizona (Cassini, PIA02873); Saturn, NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute
  (Cassini, PIA06193); Uranus and Neptune, NASA/JPL (Voyager 2, PIA18182
  and PIA01492); the Moon, NASA's Scientific Visualization Studio (Moon
  Phase and Libration 2026). Use of these images does not imply NASA's
  endorsement of this app. The same pictures, as src/pkjs/page_art.js,
  show on the settings page's logbook.

- Place names in the logbook (src/pkjs/places_data.js, by
  tools/make_places.py): Natural Earth populated places
  (https://www.naturalearthdata.com), public domain; trimmed to places of
  1,000 people or more and repacked.

- Aircraft positions from adsb.lol (https://adsb.lol), Open Database
  License (ODbL), and adsb.fi (https://adsb.fi), personal non-commercial
  use. Downloaded by the app at run time; not stored in this repository.
- Airline, airport, aircraft type and route data: Virtual Radar Server
  standing data (https://github.com/vradarserver/standing-data), CC0,
  downloaded by the app at run time.
- Satellite orbital elements from CelesTrak (https://celestrak.org), drawn
  from Space-Track.org data, downloaded by the app at run time.

